Lesson 68 wrote Π(q²)=Π(0)+Π̂(q²) and absorbed Π(0) into the definition of e. What remains in the photon line is a factor 1/(1−Π̂)≈1+Π̂. At large |q²| this is the running coupling. At small |q²| it is a short-range modification of Coulomb's law.

# 2 Small-q² expansion

## 2.1 Why the first term is q²/m²

Π̂(0)=0 by construction. Analyticity in q² near 0, and the absence of a massless charged particle that could produce a branch cut at q²=0, imply Π̂(q²)=c q²/m²+O(q⁴/m⁴). The coefficient c is a finite Feynman-parameter integral over Lesson 62's integrand. Its value is

$$ Π̂(q²) = (α / 15π) (q² / m²) + O(q⁴/m⁴) , qquad |q²| ≪ m²

### Worked example 1

Check dimensions. Π̂ is dimensionless, q²/m² is dimensionless, α is dimensionless, so c is a pure number. The 15 in the denominator is 1/5 of the 3 in Lesson 68's (α/3π) log, coming from the first two moments of the same spectral function: the log is the large-q² end, 1/15 is the small-q² end.

## 2.2 From Π̂ to a potential

In momentum space the Coulomb interaction is 4πα/q² (Heaviside–Lorentz, α=e²/4π). Replacing 1/q² by (1+Π̂)/q² adds

$$ ΔṼ(q) = (4π α / q²) Π̂(q²) ≈ 4π α (α / 15π) (1/m²) = (4α² / 15) (1/m²)

a q-independent constant. The Fourier transform of a constant in three-momentum is a contact term:

$$ ΔV(r) = − (4α² / 15 m²) δ³(r)

up to the sign convention that a negative potential is attractive. (The overall sign follows because Π̂>0 at spacelike q² in our metric, which screens the charge at long distance and therefore concentrates extra attraction at r≲1/m.) The true Uehling potential is this contact term smeared over a Compton wavelength, not a mathematical δ³(r); for atomic expectation values in states whose support is a₀≫1/m, the contact approximation is enough.

# 3 The 2s shift

$$ δE_U(ns) = ⟨ns|ΔV|ns⟩ = − (4α² / 15 m²) |ψ_{ns}(0)|²

Using |ψ_{2s}(0)|²=(αm)³/(8π) from Lesson 76,

$$ δE_U(2s) = − (4α² / 15 m²) (α³ m³ / 8π) = − (α⁴ m / 30π) α

which is of order 10 MHz. The standard evaluation of the unsmeared-plus-Uehling integral for hydrogen 2s−2p is −27.13 MHz. Because 2p has ψ(0)=0, this piece, like Lesson 76's contact self-energy, is almost entirely a 2s shift. It lowers 2s, which shrinks the Lamb interval: vacuum polarization works against Bethe's self-energy logarithm.

### Worked example 2

State the sign in words. Vacuum polarization screens: at distances ≫1/m the effective charge is smaller. An s-electron, which samples r~1/m, sees extra unscreened charge and is bound more tightly, so its energy goes down. The 2s–2p Lamb interval, which had 2s above 2p from the self-energy, is reduced.

# 4 One function, two places

At |q²|≫m², the same Π̂ is Lesson 68's (α/3π) log(|q²|/m²). Precision in hydrogen and running at a collider are not two theories. They are Π̂(q²) at two values of q². Lesson 78 will put numbers on both.

