# Solutions

**1.** Π̂(0)=0 is the renormalization condition that the long-distance charge is the measured e. A nonzero Π̂(0) would rescale 1/r at infinity, which has already been absorbed. There is therefore no correction to the coefficient of 1/r at r→∞.

**2.** (4πα/q²)Π̂=(4πα/q²)(α/15π)(q²/m²)=(4α²/15)/m².

**3.** ∫d³q/(2π)³ e^{iq·r} × const = const × δ³(r). The overall 4π conventions follow the Coulomb Fourier pair 4πα/q² ↔ α/r.

**4.** δE=−(4α²/15 m²)(αm)³/(8π)=−α⁵ m/(30π), of order tens of MHz once converted with 1 eV=2.418×10¹⁴ Hz. The textbook number including the full Uehling shape is −27.13 MHz.

**5.** ψ_{2p}(0)=0, so a contact operator vanishes. The smeared Uehling tail gives a much smaller 2p piece.

**6.** Decreases it: 2s is lowered.

**7.** Π̂(q²)≈−(α/3π)log(|q²|/m²) in the mostly-minus metric conventions of Lesson 68, producing α(q²)=α/[1−(α/3π)log(|q²|/m²)].

**8.** A muon's Bohr radius is 1/(α m_μ), comparable to 1/m_e, so the Uehling cloud is no longer a point relative to the wavefunction and the shift is a large fraction of the muonic Lamb interval.

**9.** Both 3 and 15 are moments of the one-loop fermion spectral function of Π; the log is the high-q² moment, 1/15 the threshold moment.

**10.** |ψ(0)|² grows as m_μ³, so the contact estimate is larger by (m_μ/m_e)³≈200³, while the true Uehling function, varying on 1/m_e, must be integrated against the muon wavefunction. Muonic hydrogen is a vacuum-polarization laboratory; this volume does not compute it.

